---
/**
 * Privacy, in plain words, for the app, the site, the package and HD mode.
 * Every claim here is checkable in the code: the app's only request of its own
 * is the header asking who's signed in (apps/app/src/account/status.ts; its CSP
 * allows connect-src 'self' only), stores the session in
 * IndexedDB (apps/app/src/lib/session.ts) and preferences in localStorage.
 * Accounts and credits: server/ and supabase/migrations/. Paddle's review needs
 * the seller's legal name and address here, and Paddle named as a recipient.
 * Update the date (data/legal.ts) when anything here changes.
 */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import { ADDRESS, CONTACT, SELLER, SELLER_KIND, UPDATED } from '../data/legal'
---

<Page
  title="Privacy · Inlay"
  description="Inlay's free app never receives your images: they're traced in your browser. Accounts are only for buying HD credits."
>
  <article class="prose" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
    <h1 id="privacy-title">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">In the free app, your images never leave your device. Beyond an anonymous page-view count, we only learn anything about you if you buy HD credits.</p>

    <h2>Who's responsible</h2>
    <p>
      Inlay is operated by {SELLER}, {SELLER_KIND}, who is responsible for the personal data described here.
      Postal address: {ADDRESS}.
      Questions or requests about your data: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The free app: your images stay with you</h2>
    <p>
      When you open an image in the <a href="/app/">app</a>, your browser decodes it, traces it, lets you edit it and exports the result,
      all on your device. Nothing is uploaded, and we never receive, see or store your files. Apart from loading its own code, the app
      makes small requests to this site: one to show whether you're signed in and how many HD credits you have, and others that count
      the page view and each finished trace and download. None carries an image. Its security policy stops its code from sending data
      anywhere else.
    </p>
    <p>
      The optional in-browser AI steps (removing the background, upscaling small images) work the same way: the models are downloaded from
      this site the first time you use them and run in your browser, on your image, on your device.
    </p>
    <p>
      So a reload or a closed tab doesn't lose your work, the app keeps your current image, its settings and your edits in your browser's own
      storage, along with your theme and overlay colours. That copy is on your device only: we can't see it. <strong>Start over</strong> in
      the app deletes it, and so does clearing this site's data in your browser.
    </p>

    <h2>Accounts and HD credits</h2>
    <p>You only need an account to buy and use HD credits. If you make one, we keep:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your email address, to sign you in (by emailed link; there's no password) and to match your purchases.</li>
      <li>Your credit balance and its history: purchases, refunds and HD runs, each with the credits, the image's size and the date. Never the image.</li>
      <li>Sign-in records (when you signed in, and the IP address and browser used), to keep accounts secure.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Signing in sets two cookies that only this site's server can read, to keep you signed in; they aren't used for anything else. We need
      this data to provide the service you've asked for. You can delete your account, with its history, at any time from the account page.
    </p>

    <h2>HD mode</h2>
    <p>
      HD mode (in beta) is the one part of Inlay where an image leaves your device, and only when you choose it for that image. The
      image is uploaded to our server, processed, and deleted as soon as the result is sent back to you. We don't keep it, look at it or use
      it for anything else, including training.
    </p>

    <h2>Payments</h2>
    <p>
      Credits are sold by Paddle, our reseller and the Merchant of Record. When you buy, you give your payment details, email and billing
      country (and sometimes address or tax number) to Paddle, not to us; Paddle handles them under its <a
        href="https://www.paddle.com/legal/privacy">privacy policy</a> and keeps records of the sale as tax law requires. We receive the
      order's details from Paddle (your email, the pack, the amount and its id), never your card.
    </p>

    <h2>Who else handles data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Paddle</strong>: payments, receipts, refunds and invoices.</li>
      <li><strong>Vercel</strong>: hosts the site and runs its server code. Like any host, it records basic details of each request (such as IP address, browser and page) to deliver the site and keep it secure. It also counts page views (Web Analytics; see below). See <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy">Vercel's privacy policy</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Supabase</strong>: stores accounts and credit balances.</li>
      <li><strong>Resend</strong>: sends the sign-in emails.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These providers process the data on our behalf, in the United States, and only to run Inlay. We'd also share data if the law required
      it. We never sell it.
    </p>

    <h2>What we don't do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No tracking or advertising. We count page views with Vercel Web Analytics, which sets no cookies and records only the time,
        the page, the referring site, any utm_ campaign tags in the link, and your country, browser, operating system and device type.
        It recognises a repeat visit within a day by a hash it then discards, and never sees your images. Its script is written by
        Vercel and served from this site.
      </li>
      <li>
        We also keep daily totals on mattblack.space, the site of Inlay's maker: page views, visits (with only the kind of place you came
        from, such as a search engine, LinkedIn or another site), and how many images are traced and downloaded. Each just adds one to
        that day's count. No cookie, IP address or identifier is kept, nor which page you were on or anything about your image.
      </li>
      <li>No third-party scripts or fonts, except Paddle's checkout on the buy page.</li>
      <li>No cookies unless you sign in.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>
      Account data stays until you delete your account. Sign-in links expire after an hour. HD images are deleted as soon as the result is
      back. Paddle keeps its records of a sale for as long as tax law requires.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask to see, correct, export or delete the personal data we hold about you, or object to how it's used, by emailing <a
        href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. Deleting your account from the account page removes it straight away. Depending on where you
      live, you can also complain to your data protection authority.
    </p>

    <h2>The npm package</h2>
    <p>
      <code>inlaytrace</code> runs inside your own code and makes no network requests. What happens to the images you trace with it is up to
      your application.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. Inlay is open source, so you can also <a href="https://github.com/mtblck/inlaytrace"
        >read the code</a> to check any of this.
    </p>

    <p class="fine">Last updated {UPDATED}.</p>
  </article>
</Page>
